<% content_for :title, "Agent skill — Shadwire" %>
<% content_for :description, "The skill that gives coding agents like Claude Code, Codex and Cursor context about Shadwire's CLI and components." %>
<% content_for :head do %>
  <%= tag.style rouge_stylesheet %>
<% end %>

<article class="space-y-12">
  <%= render "docs/header",
        title: "Agent skill",
        description: "A skill you add to your coding agent so it knows how to use Shadwire: the workflow, the rules, and the components your app actually has." %>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <p class="leading-7">
      Without context, an agent writing ERB makes things up: it passes <%= docs_code("icon:") %>
      to a button, calls <%= docs_code("ui_carousel") %> in an app that does not have the
      carousel installed, or writes <%= docs_code("bg-blue-500") %> where a token exists. The
      skill teaches the agent the workflow and the conventions. For the details (component
      names, variants, arguments, helpers) it has the agent ask the CLI, so the answers match
      your app.
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:install], language: :shell) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The <%= link_to "skills", "https://github.com/obra/superpowers", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener", class: "font-medium underline underline-offset-4" %>
      installer works with Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, OpenCode, Gemini CLI and about twenty
      other agents. The skill's source is in <%= link_to "skills/shadwire/", "https://github.com/edumoraes/shadwire/tree/main/skills/shadwire", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener", class: "font-medium underline underline-offset-4" %>
      in the repository.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("The skill does not list components") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      If the skill listed the components and their variants, the list would be out of date
      after your first <%= docs_code("add") %>, and nothing would tell you. Instead, it loads the
      output of <%= docs_code("bin/shadwire status --json") %> into the agent's context and tells
      the agent to read it first.
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:context], language: :markdown) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      That command always returns valid JSON. Three of its fields matter most for what the
      agent writes:
    </p>

    <%= render "docs/table",
          headers: [ "Field", "Why it matters" ],
          rows: [
            [ docs_code("installed[].helpers"), "The ui_* methods that exist in this app. Calling the helper of a component that is not installed raises NoMethodError." ],
            [ docs_code("stack.importmap"), "Whether the interactive components can work. #{registry_stimulus_component_count} of the #{docs_component_items.size} components ship a Stimulus controller." ],
            [ docs_code("cli.binstub"), safe_join([ "Whether ", docs_code("bin/shadwire"), " exists. If it is false, the agent runs ", docs_code("init"), " before anything else." ]) ]
          ] %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      In the Shadwire repository, CI checks that every helper and command the skill mentions
      still exists, and runs the workflow it describes from start to finish. If the skill falls
      out of date, the build fails.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("What's in it") %>

    <%= render "docs/table",
          headers: [ "File", "Contents" ],
          rows: @skill_files.map { |file| [ docs_code(file[:path]), file[:summary] ] } %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      <%= docs_code("SKILL.md") %> is the entry point, and the other files are references it
      links to. The same rules are covered on this site under
      <%= link_to "Composition", docs_composition_path, class: "font-medium underline underline-offset-4" %>,
      <%= link_to "Styling", docs_styling_path, class: "font-medium underline underline-offset-4" %>,
      <%= link_to "Forms", docs_forms_path, class: "font-medium underline underline-offset-4" %>
      and <%= link_to "Icons", docs_icons_path, class: "font-medium underline underline-offset-4" %>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("The workflow it teaches") %>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:workflow], language: :shell) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      <%= docs_code("info --json") %> returns the helper names, variants and sizes. Agents skip
      this step more than any other, and without it they rebuild the component out of
      <%= docs_code("div") %>s and utility classes.
    </p>

    <%= render layout: "docs/callout", locals: { icon: "info", title: "Trust the exit code" } do %>
      A non-zero exit code means something was not applied (usually a <code>bundle add</code>),
      and the message says how to fix it. The skill tells the agent to stop at that point
      instead of carrying on as if it had worked.
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Permissions") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The skill's frontmatter declares the tools it needs:
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:frontmatter], language: :yaml) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      <%= docs_code("allowed-tools") %> only applies to the turn in which the skill is loaded.
      To stop the permission prompts for good, add a rule to the app's
      <%= docs_code(".claude/settings.json") %>:
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:permissions], language: :json) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      <%= docs_code("user-invocable: false") %> is on purpose: you don't call this skill
      yourself. The agent loads it when it works on UI in a Rails app that has a
      <%= docs_code("shadwire.json") %>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Agents without the skill") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Not every agent supports skills. The same catalog is also published as plain text, in the
      <%= link_to "llms.txt", docs_llms_txt_path, class: "font-medium underline underline-offset-4" %>
      format: an index that says when to use each component, and a file with the full API. You
      can paste either one into a model's context.
    </p>
  </section>
</article>
